title: Confirming a trashing
tags: Trash
kind: reference
aliases: confirm-trash
summary: What touches several files or metarecords always asks first; trashing one item asks unless confirm-trash = false in the GUI's config.toml; deleting one metarecord never asks.

Three rules, from the most to the least at stake.

''What touches several files or metarecords at once always asks'', naming what
goes or how many: keeping one duplicate (<<cmd "duplicates:keep">>), <<cmd "file:trash">>
on several files, `item:delete selection` / `query`, `metarecord:bulk … trash`
and `… delete`, the list's bulk form, `orphan:delete` ([[Sending files to the trash]], [[Orphans]]).
Undo or the trash gives everything back, but a set is easily larger than one
meant, and the count is what tells.

''Trashing one item asks, unless switched off'': Delete on a file
(<<cmd "item:delete">>, and ''Move to trash'' in a context menu, which runs it),
<<cmd "file:trash">> on one file. A trashing is never final — the repository's
trash gives the file and its metarecords back, the desktop's the file
([[The desktop trash]]) — so the question is a safety rather than a necessity,
on by default, and dropped in the GUI's `config.toml` ([[GUI configuration]]):

```toml
[panels]
confirm-trash = false
```

''Deleting one metarecord never asks'': Delete on a metarecord without a file,
<<cmd "metarecord:delete">>. Nothing goes to a trash, and
<<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>> brings it back.

What is final keeps its own question whatever the rest says: pruning the trash.
